## 13. Offline PDF Downloads

Offline reading is one of the most important features.

I need to read textbooks when I am away from home without internet access.

### Download workflow

Every PDF should have a clear download control.

When I download a document:
1. Save the complete PDF to persistent local device storage.
2. Show progress when feasible.
3. Handle interruption and retry.
4. Verify the file is usable before marking it available offline.
5. Display an Available Offline status only after a successful download.
6. Show the document in Downloaded for Offline.

Never claim offline availability if the file has not been fully downloaded.

### Offline functionality

In airplane mode, a downloaded PDF must allow me to:
- Open and read the document.
- Navigate through its pages.
- Zoom.
- Search when supported by the document.
- Restore reading position.
- Open bookmarks.
- View existing highlights.
- Create or edit supported highlights and annotations.
- Save reading progress and personal notes.

Changes made offline must be saved locally.

If synchronization is available, queue offline changes and synchronize them when connectivity returns. Resolve conflicts safely rather than silently discarding changes.

### Storage management

Display:
- Download status.
- File size.
- Total local storage usage where available.
- A way to remove a local copy.
- A way to download the document again.
- Warnings when storage is insufficient.

Removing a local copy must not delete the cloud original or its associated annotations.

Keep offline copies independent on each device unless I explicitly transfer them.

